 ## Overview
-This method modifies the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting rasterization pipeline to inject a custom world-to-camera transformation matrix (`camera_pose`) during rendering [S1, unofficial]. It transforms the Gaussian 3D means and rotations into the new camera frame while keeping the primitives fixed in their original world-space coordinates [S1, unofficial]. This approach handles the coordinate frame transformation required for novel view synthesis or pose correction, distinct from tile-based memory management methods [S1, unofficial].
+This method modifies the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting rasterization pipeline to allow the camera pose to be overridden by an external tensor during rendering, rather than using the stored viewpoint parameters. It solves the problem of rendering a pre-trained Gaussian model from arbitrary or dynamically computed camera positions (e.g., from a SLAM system or a differentiable reconstruction loop) without retraining the model. It sets apart from standard rendering by injecting a custom world-to-camera transformation (`rel_w2c`) and quaternion rotation into the projection and covariance computation steps, enabling differentiable rendering with respect to the camera pose [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method intercepts the rasterization call, computes a relative world-to-camera matrix from the override pose, transforms the Gaussian attributes (means and rotations) into this new camera frame, and passes them to the rasterizer [S1, unofficial].
-
