-### Parameters and Typical Values
-- `camera_pose`: The specific pose to render from. No default; must be provided [S1, unofficial].
-- `pipe.compute_cov3D_python`: Boolean. If true, covariances are computed in Python; otherwise, scales and rotations are passed separately [S1, unofficial]. Default is false [S1, unofficial].
-- `pipe.convert_SHs_python`: Boolean. If true, SHs are converted to RGB in Python; otherwise, passed to rasterizer [S1, unofficial]. Default is false [S1, unofficial].
+**Parameters:**
+- `camera_pose`: The override pose. Must be compatible with `get_camera_from_tensor` [S1, unofficial].
+- `pipe.compute_cov3D_python`: Boolean. If true, covariance is computed in Python; otherwise, on GPU [S1, unofficial].
+- `pipe.convert_SHs_python`: Boolean. If true, SH to RGB conversion is done in Python; otherwise, on GPU [S1, unofficial].
 
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Precision**: Ensure `camera_pose` is on the correct device (CUDA) and dtype matches the Gaussian attributes [S1, unofficial].
-- **Boundary Conditions**: If `camera_pose` results in Gaussians being behind the camera, the rasterizer handles culling; the visibility filter (`radii > 0`) identifies valid Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-- **Degenerate Input**: If `camera_pose` is singular or invalid, the transformation will fail; validate `camera_pose` before use [G].
-- **Gradient Flow**: The `screenspace_points` tensor is initialized to zero and retains gradients, which is crucial for backpropagation if the camera pose is optimized in a training loop [S1, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Gradient Flow:** `screenspace_points` must have `requires_grad=True` and `retain_grad()` called to ensure gradients can flow back to the 2D means during backpropagation [S1, unofficial].
+- **Rotation Convention:** `quadmultiply` must handle the specific quaternion convention (wxyz vs xyzw) used by the Gaussian storage and the `camera_pose` tensor [S1, unofficial].
+- **Direction Vectors:** When computing SH colors in Python, the direction vectors must be normalized and computed in the correct coordinate system (camera space) [S1, unofficial].
